; STORE-SHELL §5 check 2, VALUE plane (W3-12 + W3-9) — `r2-17`'s shape, corrected.
;
; A-3 said duplicate-key freedom on the value plane was "a boundary check"; F-33 showed
; the boundary had no such check, and `r2-17` pinned it: the same `canonVFields`
; involution admits a palindromic duplicate-key `vobj`, `check` calls the store clean,
; and `resolve` hands it back. W3-9 moved the clause onto `Reachable.putE`'s premise list
; and W3-12 wired the boundary to `E2.valueAdmissionClause`, which IS that premise.
;
; The rejection names `dup-key-value` rather than `dup-key`: one verdict for both planes
; would leave this fixture unable to tell a value-plane fix from a schema-plane one.

; a record schema, so the value's keys are unconstrained by the schema
(schema-put (record (prim int)))
(assert-code prev 0)

; the identical pair
(entity-put @1 (obj ("a" (i 1)) ("a" (i 1))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; the palindromic triple carrying two different values at one key
(entity-put @1 (obj ("a" (i 1)) ("a" (i 2)) ("a" (i 1))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; nested inside an array inside an object: the clause descends both value constructors
(entity-put @1 (obj ("outer" (arr (obj ("k" (i 1)) ("k" (i 2)))))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; distinct keys are admitted
(entity-put @1 (obj ("a" (i 1)) ("b" (i 2))))
(assert-code prev 0)

; the schema and the one admitted entity, and nothing else.
(check)
